l  and if this is the angle theta we see  that a parallel here and I'll write it  as a parallel is the magnitude of a  times cosine of theta and so I can also  define a dot B as equal to how much of a  is parallel to B times the magnitude of  B now in both of these instances because  B parallel is the magnitude of B cosine  theta and a pal is the magnitude of a  cosine theta what we're writing is is  that a dot B is the magnitude of a  cosine theta magnitude of B and now you  can see that this quantity is a parallel  and this quantity is B parallel and this  is our geometric definition of a dot  product now one thing that we want to  consider are two important rules for dot  products and they're the following that  if we take a vector and multiply a by a  quantity as  see here is a scalar quantity then this  is equal to C times a dot B now  geometrically this is very easy to see  because if we draw a and we draw B and  if I multiply a by a scalar and let's  make in this particular picture our  scalar bigger than 1 then the new vector  a is multiplied by C and it's C times a  and the scalar product is just see a dot  B is the magnitude of C a times how much  of B is parallel and when you multiply  this together you get C times a times B  parallel now C of a dot B is equal to C  times the magnitude of a times B  parallel and you can see that these two  expressions are equal so if you multiply  a dot product by a scalar it satisfies  this rule and there's one other crucial  rule that we'll need when we look at  vectors that if you take a vector a and  you add to it a vector B and you dot it  into a vector C then that's this is  vector addition and vector  multiplication this vector addition  distributes over vector multiplication  by a dot C plus B dot C so these two  facts are crucial to our development of  vectors and one thing that we can say is  we'll leave this as a little exercise  for you to prove this result you  is pretty straightforward vector  construction a